        "id": "p91-q52-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the equation of a line with gradient $m$ passing through $P$ is $y=mx-ma+\\frac{k}{a}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p91-q52-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=mx-ma+\\frac{k}{a}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p91-q52-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use point-gradient form: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y-\\frac{k}{a}=m(x-a)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Using point-gradient form at $P$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y-\\frac{k}{a}&=m(x-a)\\\\y&=mx-ma+\\frac{k}{a}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p91-q52-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Given the line is a tangent, show that $m=-\\frac{k}{a^2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p91-q52-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "m=-\\frac{k}{a^2}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p91-q52-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Set the intersection discriminant to zero: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\left(ma+\\frac{k}{a}\\right)^2=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Here $k\\ne0$ and $a\\ne0$. At an intersection,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{k}{x}&=mx-ma+\\frac{k}{a}\\\\mx^2+\\left(\\frac{k}{a}-ma\\right)x-k&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nIf $m=0$, this equation is linear with the simple root $x=a$, so the line is not tangent. For tangency, the quadratic has a repeated root and $\\Delta=0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac{k}{a}-ma\\right)^2+4mk&=0\\\\\\left(ma+\\frac{k}{a}\\right)^2&=0\\\\ma&=-\\frac{k}{a}\\\\m&=-\\frac{k}{a^2}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      }
    ]
